                  %a{:href => "#kaluzaklein_compactifications"} Kaluza-Klein compactifications
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#via_adscft"} Via AdS/CFT
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#more"} More
              %li
                %a{:href => "#TheOpenProblem"} The open problem of formulating M-theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#OpenProblemGeneral"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#OpenProblemNonabelianDBI"} Non-abelian DBI-action
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#OpenProblemM5BraneAnomalyCancellation"} M5-Brane anomaly cancellation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#OpenProblemCoincidentM5Branes"} Coincident M5-branes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general_2"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_adscft"} Relation to AdS/CFT
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cohomological_considerations"} Cohomological considerations
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_dbrane_mechanics"}
                    Relation to D
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                    \-brane mechanics
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#more_on_the_relation_to_type_iia_string_theory"} More on the relation to type IIA string theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_terms_of_higher_geometry"} In terms of higher geometry
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#activity"} Activity
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There are various hints (originally observed in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten95"} Witten 95
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring+theories"} superstring theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II
            (A and B),
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+string+theory"} type I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 32
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(32)
            and
            = succeed "))" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 8
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 8
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8 \times E_8
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} strong coupling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            limit whose low energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            description is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            and which reduces to the various string theories by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            torus bundle, followed by various
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} string dualities
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} membrane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            in 11-dimensions after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            this hypothetical theory has been called “M-theory” short for “membrane theory”; e.g. in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#HoravaWitten95"} Horava-Witten 95
          %blockquote
            %p
              As it has been proposed that the eleven-dimensional theory is a supermembrane theory but there are some reasons to doubt that interpretation, we will non-committally call it the
              = succeed "," do
                %em M-theory
          %p
            The “reasons to doubt” that interpretation is that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            does not support a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            the way that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            does. This is part of the reason why the actual nature of “M-theory” remains mysterious. On the other hand, later it was argued that there is a regularization of the M2-brane worldvolume theory, which makes it becomes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BFSS+matrix+model"} BFSS matrix model
            (
            %a{:href => "#NicolaiHelling98"}> Nicolai-Helling 98
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DasguptaNicolaiPlefka02"} Dasgupta-Nicolai-Plefka 02
          %blockquote
            %p#MagicMysteryQuote
              Later, the membranes were interpreted in terms of matrices. Purely by chance, the word “matrix” also starts with “m”, so for a while I would say that the M stands for magic, mystery, or matrix. (
              %a{:href => "#Witten14"}> Witten 2014, last paragraph
              )
          %p
            The defining characteristic of M-theory is that it exhibits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality with type IIA string theory
            in the following way:
          #eq:MTheoryDiagram.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                          %mo −
                          %mi Theory
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo ?
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi low
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi energy
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi limit
                        %mtd
                          %mn 11
                          %mi d
                          %mi Supergravity
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtable
                                  %mtr
                                    %mtd
                                      %mi small
                                  %mtr
                                    %mtd
                                      %mi coupling
                                  %mtr
                                    %mtd
                                      %mi limit
                          %mo{:maxsize => "3em", :minsize => "3em"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "3em", :minsize => "3em"} ↓
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtable
                                  %mtr
                                    %mtd
                                      %mi KK
                                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                      %mi reduction
                                  %mtr
                                    %mtd
                                      %mi on
                                      %msup
                                        %mi S
                                        %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi type
                          %mi IIA
                          %mi string
                          %mi theory
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi low
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi energy
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi effective
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi QFT
                        %mtd
                          %mn 10
                          %mi d
                          %mi Supergravity
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  M-Theory(?) &amp;\stackrel{low\;energy\;limit}{\to}&amp; 11d Supergravity
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{ \array{ small \\ coupling \\ limit}}}
                  \\Bigg\downarrow
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\Bigg\downarrow
                  {}^{\mathrlap{ \array{KK\;reduction \\ on S^1 }}}
                  \\\
                  type IIA string theory
                  \&amp;\stackrel{low\;energy\;effective\;QFT}{\to}&amp;
                  10d Supergravity
                  }
          %p
            (see also e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#ObersPioline98"}> Obers-Pioline 98, p. 12
            )). The unknown top left corner here has optimistically been given a name, and that is “M-theory”. But even the rough global structure of the top left corner has remained elusive.
          %blockquote
            %p
              We still have no fundamental formulation of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
              ” - the hypothetical theory of which
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
              and the five
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theories"} string theories
              are all special limiting cases. Work on formulating the fundamental principles underlying M-theory has noticeably waned.
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "Physical+Mathematics+and+the+Future#Moore14"}> Moore 14, section 12
              )
          %h2#Hints Hints for M-theory
          %p
            The available evidence that there is something like M-theory consists of various facets in the bottom left and the top right entry of the above diagram
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:MTheoryDiagram"} (1)
            that seem to have a common origin in the top left corner.
          %h3#membranes Membranes
          %p
            Notably, from the
            = succeed "-solution" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            and from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
            one finds that it contains a 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"}> brane
            , called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            does for the bottom left corner. At the bottom of it, this is simply because, by the very nature of the conjecture, the top left corner is supposed to be given by a non-perturbative strong-coupling limit of the bottom left corner. But one may also see that the evident guess for a would-be membrane analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            fails
          %blockquote
            %p#WittenOnProblemsOfMembraneTheory2014
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Duff"}> Mike Duff
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Townsend"} Paul Townsend
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermembranes"} supermembranes
              had spent a couple of years in the mid-1980s saying that there should be a theory of fundamental membranes analogous to the theory of fundamental strings. That wasn’t convincing for a large number of reasons. For one thing, a three-manifold doesn’t have an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+characteristic"} Euler characteristic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+invariance"} conformal invariance
              to help us make sense of membrane theory; membrane theory is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} nonrenormalizable
              just like
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            %p
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              in interview by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hirosi+Ooguri"} Hirosi Ooguri
              = succeed "))" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/201505/rnoti-p491.pdf"} pdf
          %p This issue is the very root of the abbreviation “M-theory”:
          %blockquote
            %p
              As it has been proposed that the eleven-dimensional theory is a supermembrane theory but there are some reasons to doubt that interpretation, we will non-committally call it the
              = succeed "," do
                %em M-theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#HoravaWitten95"} Horava-Witten 95
            %p#Witten14LastParagraph
              M-theory was meant as a temporary name pending a better understanding. Some colleagues thought that the theory should be understood as a membrane theory. Though I was skeptical, I decided to keep the letter “m” from “membrane” and call the theory M–theory, with time to tell whether the M stands for magic, mystery, or membrane. Later, the membranes were interpreted in terms of matrices. Purely by chance, the word “matrix” also starts with “m”, so for a while I would say that the M stands for magic, mystery, or matrix. (
              %a{:href => "#Witten14"}> Witten 14, last paragraph
              )
          %h3#StronglyCoupledTypeIIAAndD0Branes
            Strongly coupled type IIA strings and D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            \-branes
          %p
            There is a bunch of consistency checks on the statement that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            on a circle gives the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} strong coupling
            refinement of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type IIA string theory
          %p
            One aspect of this is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensate"} condensate
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D0-branes"} D0-branes
            behaves like a 10-dimensional theory that develops a further circular dimension of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
            scaling with the density of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D0-branes"} D0-branes
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BanksFischlerShenkerSusskind97"} Banks-Fischler-Shenker-Susskind 97
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Polchinski99"} Polchinski 99
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FSS13"} FSS 13, section 4.2
          %p
            Discussion of the relation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+enhancement"} gauge enhancement
            of M-theory at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+singularities"} ADE singularities
            and the corresponding coincident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            geometries in type IIA string theory is in (
            %a{:href => "#Sen97"}> Sen 97
            ).
          %p
            More on the decomposition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            in 11d to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            and the NS-fields in type IIA is in (
            %a{:href => "#MathaiSati03"}> Mathai-Sati 03, section 4
            ).
          %p
            For survey of how the components maps see also the table at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#RelationToFTheory"} Relation to F-theory
          %h3#uduality U-duality
          %p
            Another hint comes from the fact that the
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"} U-duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theories forms a clear pattern in those dimensions where one understands it well, giving rise to a description of higher dimensional supergravity theories by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+generalized+geometry"} exceptional generalized geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+generalized+geometry"} exceptional generalized geometry
            over
            %em hyperbolic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Moody+Lie+algebras"} Kac-Moody Lie algebras
            such as first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E10"} E10
            and then, ultimately
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E11"} E11
            %em is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 11
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{11}
            in some way. But it all remains rather mysterious at the moment.
          %h3#RelationToFTheory Relation to F-theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactification
            of M-theory on a torus yields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            – directly type IIA, and then type IIB after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-dualizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axio-dilaton"} axio-dilaton
            of the resulting type II-B string theory is equivalently the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli"} modulus
            of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+fibration"} elliptic fibration
            by the compactification torus. This gives a description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            aspects of type II which has come to be known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            (see e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Johnson97"} Johnson 97
          %p
            In slightly more detail, write, topologically,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^2 = S^1_A\times S^1_B
            for the compactification torus of M-theory, where contracting the first
            = succeed "-factor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_B
            is to be compactified (since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            sends the radius
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_A
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_A
            to the inverse radius
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mi s
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_B = \ell_s^2 / R_A
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 10
            is obtained from 11d sugra compactified on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_A \times S^1_B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            and this may vary over the 9d base space as an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+fibration"} elliptic fibration
          %p
            Applying T-duality to one of the compact direction yields a 10-dimensional theory which may now be thought of as encoded by a 12-dimensional elliptic fibration. This 12d elliptic fibration encoding a 10d type II supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            is the input data that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            is concerned with.
          %p A schematic depiction of this story is the following:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                %th
                %th
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11d+supergravity"} M-theory
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mn 11
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 11
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  F-theory in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mn 12
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 12
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \downarrow
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} KK-reduction
                  along
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+fibration"} elliptic fibration
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \downarrow
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axio-dilaton"} axio-dilaton
                  is modulus of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+fibration"} elliptic fibration
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} IIA string theory
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mn 9
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 9
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ←
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftarrow
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rightarrow
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} IIB string theory
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo =
                        %mn 10
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 10
          %p
            In the simple case where the elliptic fiber is indeed just
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_A \times S^1_B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/imaginary+part"} imaginary part
            of its complex modulus is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Im(\tau) = \frac{R_A}{R_B}
                  \\,.
          %p
            By following through the above diagram, one finds how this determines the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %p
            First, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            of M-theory on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_A
            yields a type IIA
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+coupling+constant"} string coupling constant
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi IIA
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g_{IIA} = \frac{R_A}{\ell_s}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then the T-duality operation along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_B
            divides this by
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mi IIB
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mi IIA
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ℓ
                                %mi s
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi R
                                %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi R
                                %mi A
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi R
                                %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi Im
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi τ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  g_{IIB} &amp; = g_{IIA} \frac{\ell_s}{R_B}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = \frac{R_A}{R_B}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = Im(\tau)
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %div
            %p
