          %h3#via_adscft Via AdS/CFT
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            may be turned around to deduce from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            in the 11-dimensional
            = succeed "-spacetime," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            is not completely known,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+invariance"} conformal invariance
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            tightly constrains it (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+bootstrap"}> conformal bootstrap
            ”) and does allow to extract results.
          %p
            This approach to computing putative M-theory scattering amplitudes is due to (
            %a{:href => "#ChesterPerlmutter18"}> ChesterPerlmutter18
            ).
          %h3#more More
          %p (…)
          %h2#TheOpenProblem The open problem of formulating M-theory
          %p
            The tight web of hints and plausiblity checks notwithstanding, an actual formulation of M-theory as an actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            remains an open problem.
          %p
            This is not outrageous in itself: In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            there are good examples of cases where a collection of situations was or is suspected to be limiting cases of a single unified theory, without that theory itself having been or being known.
          %p
            One example of this is the putative theory “absolute geometry over the
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theories"} perturbative string theories
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"}> HET
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+string+theory"} I
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} IIA
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} IIB
            and their
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactifications"} KK-compactifications
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometries
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+field"} prime field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \geq 2
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
          %p
            On the other hand, parts of the physics-minded literature tends to forget or downplay the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjectural
            nature of many assumptions or leaps of faiths that are being made when it comes to discussion of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-brane"} M-brane
            dynamics and generally of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            in string theory.
          %p The following is a collection of quotes from authors that highlight the open problem:
          %h3#OpenProblemGeneral General
          %p#Witten1988Quote
            %a{:href => "Edward+Witten#DavisBrown88"} Witten 1988
            (before the notion of M-theory was formulated):
          %blockquote
            %p
              String theory at its finest is, or should be, a new branch of geometry.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\ldots]
              I would consider trying to elucidate this proper generalization of geometry as the central problem of physics, certainly the central problem of string theory.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\ldots]
              What should have happened, by rights, is that the correct mathematical structures should have been developed in the twenty-first or twenty-second century, and then finally physicists should have invented string theory as a physical theory that is made possible by these structures.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\ldots]
              we are paying the price for the fact that we didn’t come by this thing in the usual way.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Duff96"} Duff 1996, totality of Section 6
            (right after the idea of M-theory was formulated):
          %blockquote
            %p The overriding problem in superunification in the coming years will be to take the Mystery out of M-theory, while keeping the Magic and the Membranes.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "DpDp2-brane+bound+state#HoweLambertWest97"}> Howe-Lambert-West 97, p. 2
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              the still rather mysterious M theory governs many aspects of the lower dimensional string theories. What little is known of M theory is powerful enough to lead us to new phenomena in string theory and indeed new string theories. In particular the M theory fivebrane is strongly believed hold a new kind of self-dual string theory on its worldvolume. This new and somewhat elusive theory has also appeared in other contexts such as the compactification of type IIB string theory on K3 , M(atrix) theory on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^5
              and the S-duality of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = 4
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4
              super-Yang-Mills. Thus one may hope that a greater understanding of this self-dual string may lead directly to a greater understanding of duality, string theory and M theory.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Duff98"}> Duff 98a, last paragraph (p. 6)
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p Despite all these successes, physicists are glimpsing only small corners of M-theory; the big picture is still lacking.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p Indeed future historians may judge the late 20th century as a time when theorists were like children playing on the seashore, diverting themselves with the smoother pebbles or prettier shells of superstrings while the great ocean of M-theory lay undiscovered before them.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Duff98b"}> Duff 98b, p. 6
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p we are only just beginning to scratch the surface of the ultimate meaning of M-theory, and for the time being therefore, M stands for Magic and Mystery too.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "BFSS+matrix+model#NicolaiHelling98"}> Nicolai-Helling 98, p. 2
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Despite the recent excitement, however, we do not think that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BFSS+matrix+model"} M(atrix) theory
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} d= 11 supermembrane
              in their present incarnation are already the final answer in the search for
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-Theory
              %em flat
              transverse dimensions only). Furthermore, there should exist some huge and so far completely hidden symmetries generalizing not only the duality symmetries of extended supergravity and string theory, but also the principles underlying general relativity.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Duff99"}> Duff 99, p.330
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p future historians may judge the period 1984-95 as a time when theorists were like boys playing by the sea shore, and diverting themselves with the smoother pebbles or prettier shells of perturbative ten-dimensiorial superstrings while the great ocean of non-perturbative eleven-dimensional M-theory lay all undiscovered before them.
          %p
            %br/
          %p#CGNT04
            %a{:href => "higher+curvature+correction#CGNT04"}> Cederwall-Gran-Nilsson-Tsimpis 04
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Our understanding of M-theory is still very limited, mainly due to the lack of powerful methods to probe it at the microscopic level. One approach to encoding information about M-theory is through its low energy
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              The ultimate goal is to be able to derive the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+curvature+corrections"} higher derivative corrections
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superspace"} superspace
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identities"} Bianchi identities
              in order to obtain the most general form such correction terms can take restricted only by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz invariance
              in eleven dimensions. To what extent such an approach can capture main features of M-theory is an interesting question to which we have no answer at this point.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Carlevaro06"}> Carlevaro 2006, p 5
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p Despite these successes, M-theory remains somewhat elusive and is still in want of a complete and closed formulation. In particular, its fundamental degrees of freedom are still unknown, which in principle prevents us from establishing the full quantum theory.
          %p
            %br/
          %p#Moore14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Physical+Mathematics+and+the+Future"}> Moore 14, pages 43-44
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              We still have no fundamental formulation of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
              ” - the hypothetical theory of which
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
              and the five
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theories"} string theories
              are all special limiting cases. Work on formulating the fundamental principles underlying M-theory has noticeably waned.
            %p#AGoodStartWasGivenByTheMatrixTheory
              A good start was given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BFSS+matrix+model"} Matrix theory
              approach of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Banks"} Banks
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Willy+Fischler"} Fischler
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Shenker"} Shenker
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leonard+Susskind"} Susskind
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              of modes in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
              multiplet in 11-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
              \- even at energies sufficiently large that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes"} black holes
              should be created. (This latter phenomenon has never been explicitly demonstrated). But
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BFSS+matrix+model"} Matrix theory
              is only a beginning and does not give us the whole picture of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactifications
              were investigated. (For example, compactification on a six-dimensional torus is not very well understood at all.
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "conformal+bootstrap#ChesterPerlmutter18"}> Chester-Perlmutter 18, p. 2
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              given our utter lack of a complete description of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
              is not terribly useful for determining finite aspects of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT"} dual CFT
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+bootstrap"} conformal bootstrap
          %p
            %br/
          %p#Witten19
            %a{:href => "Edward+Witten#WhatIsMissing"}> Witten 19, USN Interview
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p I actually believe that string/M-theory is on the right track toward a deeper explanation. But at a very fundamental level it’s not well understood. And I’m not even confident that we have a good concept of what sort of thing is missing or where to find it. The reason I am not is that in hindsight it is clear the view given in the 1980s of what is missing was too narrow. Instead of discovering what we thought was missing, we broadened the picture in the 90s, in unexpected directions.
          %p
            %br/
          %p#Duff19
            %a{:href => "Michael+Duff#WeDontKnowWhatItIs"}> Duff 19, USN interview
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p The problem we face is that we have a patchwork understanding of M-theory, like a quilt. We understand this corner and that corner, but what’s lacking is the overarching big picture. So directly or indirectly, my research hopes to explain what M-theory really is. We don’t know what it is.
            %p In a certain sense, and this is not a popular statement, I think it’s premature to be asking: “What are the empirical consequences”, because it’s not yet in a mature enough state, where we can sensibly make falsifiable prediction.
          %p
            %br/
          %p#Duff20
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duff+interview+at+M-Theory-Mathematics+2020"}> Duff interview at M-Theory-Mathematics 2020
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p (06:59) But the matrix model itself was not all of M-theory; it was a corner of M-theory, and it told us certain interesting things, but there were interesting things about M-theory that it didn’t tell us.
            %p (07:13) I think we are still looking, in fact, for what M-theory really is.
            %p (07:19) We have a patchwork picture. We understand various corners. But the overarching big picture of M-theory is still waiting to be discovered, in my view.
            %p (08:12) M-theory in 1995 was very promising, and it’s taught us a lot about the fundamental interactions; but the final theory is still not with us.
            %p (12:46) I wouldn’t like to predict what the ultimate picture of M-theory will be; I imagine it will be something quite different from what we can imagine now.
            %p (16:36) That’s why I think M-theory is not yet in a mature enough stage for us to make falsifiable predictions.
            %p (16:44) We don’t understand the theory sufficiently well yet to do so.
          %p
            %br/
          %p#WittenInterviewByZierler
            %a{:href => "https://www.aip.org/history-programs/niels-bohr-library/oral-histories/46968"}> Witten, AIP interview 2022
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p String theory and M-theory have always been different. From the beginning, they were discovered by people who discovered formulas or bits and pieces of the theory without understanding what’s behind it at a more fundamental level.
            %p And what we understand now, even today, is extremely fragmentary, and I’m sure very superficial compared to what the real theory is. That’s the problem with the claim that supposedly I invented M-theory. It would make at least as much sense to say that M-theory hasn’t been invented yet. And you could also claim it had been invented before by other people.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Gross"}> David Gross
            , in concluding the
            %a{:href => "https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/academics/divisions/science/strings-conference-2025-abu-dhabi.html"} Strings 2025
            meeting (10 Jan 2025,
            %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/0QlNKjm-peY"} Day 5 afternoon session 2
            @
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "https://t.co/D5VGrHtlQB"} 1:05:48
          %blockquote
            %p It’s always surprising to me that there is not even any talk on: “What is M-Theory?” […] Why have we totally given up on trying to understand “What is M-Theory?”
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#OpenProblemNonabelianDBI Non-abelian DBI-action
          %p
            A key ingredient of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            is supposedly the physics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+branes"} intersecting branes
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+enhancement"} gauge enhancement
            in their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DBI+field+theory"} DBI field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+DBI+action"} non-abelian DBI action
            remains open:
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "Dirac-Born-Infeld+action#TaylorVanRaamsdonk99"}> Taylor-VanRaamsdonk 99, p. 1
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              For a system of multiple D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              \-branes, the world-volume action is much less well understood than for a single brane. The extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills action
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              to a full super-symmetric or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kappa-symmetry"} κ-symmetric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+Born-Infeld+action"} nonabelian Born-Infeld action
              is not known.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              the proposal by
              %a{:href => "Dirac-Born-Infeld+action#Tseytlin97"} Tseytlin 97
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              has not yet been derived from any more fundamental principles
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "Dirac-Born-Infeld+action#Schwarz01"}> Schwarz 01, Section 2
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p The explicit construction of such an action is a difficult problem that has been studied extensively (starting with [17]), but is not yet completely settled.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a{:href => "Dirac-Born-Infeld+action#Chemissany04"} Chemissany 04, p. 5 (7 of 84)
          %blockquote
            %p Several attempts to generalize the Born-Infeld action describing one D-brane to non-Abelian action describing a stack of them have been made. The proper (perhaps closed) form of it is however not known up to date.
          %h3#OpenProblemM5BraneAnomalyCancellation M5-Brane anomaly cancellation
          %p
            Another key ingredient of M-theory is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomaly+cancellation"} anomaly cancellation
            has a convoluted history (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "M5-brane#AnomalyCancellation"} there
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Harvey05"}> Harvey 05, p. 46
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              …
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              the solution is not so clear.
              = succeed "The" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              will not work for the M5-brane.
              = succeed "\u2026" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              something new is required. What this something new is, is not a priori obvious.
              = succeed "\u2026" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              = succeed "This" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              a daunting task. To my knowledge no serious attempts have been made to study the problem.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              The proposal of
              %a{:href => "M5-brane#FreedHarveyMinasianMoore98"} FHMM 98
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              probably should not be viewed as a final understanding of the problem. One would eventually hope for a microscopic formulation of M-theory which makes some of the manipulations
              = succeed "proposed" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "M5-brane#FreedHarveyMinasianMoore98"} FHMM 98
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              appear more natural.
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#OpenProblemCoincidentM5Branes Coincident M5-branes
          %p
            Formulating the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D6+N%3D%282%2C0%29+SCFT"} D=6 N=(2,0) SCFT
            expected on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coincident+brane"} coincident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-branes"} M5-branes
            (and thus the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+enhancement"} gauge enhancement
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coincident+brane"} coincident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D4-branes"} D4-branes
            under
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type IIA string theory
          %p
            %a{:href => "6dN20SCFT#Moore12"}> Moore 12, p. 77
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The IR dynamics of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              coincident M5 branes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              remains largely mysterious.
          %p
            %a{:href => "BLG+model#Lambert12"}> Lambert 12, p. 49
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p The M5-brane theory remains an important open problem.
          %p
            %a{:href => "6dN20SCFT#Hu13"}> Hu 13, p. 1
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p The low energy effective theory on multiple M2 branes has been well understood in recent years. However, the low energy effective theory on multiple M5 branes is still an open problem.
          %p
            %a{:href => "6dN20SCFT#Lambert19"}> Lambert 19, Section 3.6
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p 3.6 Open problems and wishes
            %p Let me close this discussion of M5-branes with some open problems and wish list of results:
            %p i) Provide a field definition/construction of the (2,0) theory […]
            %p
              ii) Find the mathematical structures that best capture aspects of the (2,0) theory
              = succeed "\u2026" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+1%2FN+limit"} large 1/N limit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
              %ul
